Anyone using VXT2000 X-terminals to connect to a Linux server?

The VXT2000s are set up fine - they load in host-based mode from an
Alpha running VMS (and can run an X session to the VMS box with no
problems.)  They are running 2.1 (the latest/last version) of the VXT
software and have 18MB of memory (the max).

I've got an AlphaServer 4100 with 2 400MHz processors and a half gig
of memory, running a new install of RH 7.1.  I think I've done
everything necessary to be able to connect from a remote X term,
backed up by the fact that on a couple of occasions it has actually
worked.

Mostly though it just completely hangs the Linux box when you try to
log in.  All local X processing stops (mouse hung, control panel clock
no longer updating, etc.), you can't telnet to the box, etc.  It does
present the login box just fine on the VXT and servs fonts just fine
to the VXT, it's the part where you hit "go" after putting in your
login info on the VXT where it changes the background color and then
everything back on the Alpha box hangs.

Sometimes after an insane amount of time (2-15 minutes) it will either
successfully log in, or will fail and reset the VXT window manger, in
both cases allowing local processing to resume on the Linux box.
Other times it just hangs forever.

The only sucessful logins have been with the Gnome window manager
selected; all attempts at KDE have failed, but maybe I haven't tried
enough dozens of times...

If you just use the Alpha as a personal computer, it's fine.  No
hangs, no crashes, X runs flawlessly.  It's only remote access that is
hanging it.

I did lower the local X config to the VXTs specs (1024x768x8) to make
it work.  In the long run I'd like to discover a way to make the
remote X servers use different configs, so the local one can have 16
bits of color, but for now I'm willing to suffer to make it work.

I believe VXT2.1 is using X11R5, so I could understand some apps not
working properly, but to so totally whack the Alpha box upside the
head in the login process just just seems wrong.

Duncan


