Greetings,

I recently acquired a "Digital Alpha XL 366". I intend to run Linux on
this machine and have been reading all the docs I can find about
running Linux on these machines. The docs relating to installing Linux
recommended updating the BIOS if there was an update available.

After checking the Compaq website for an updated BIOS (the box came
with AlphaBIOS 5.13) and the latest AlphaBIOS I found on Compaq's
website for the box is 5.70. Following the instructions, I put this on
to a FAT formatted floppy and flashed the BIOS from the AlphaBIOS
update menu.

The update itself seemed to go fine - after the new BIOS was loaded
and got to the screen where you're told to press enter or reboot, I
hit enter and waited a few minutes... The system appeared to be
frozen. Powering off, etc. has not helped. I can see the CD-ROM,
floppy and HDD lights flash on as they power up, but there is no video
output, nor any audio cues as to what the problem is.

It seems hardware docs for these machines are virtually non-existant
online. It seems that what I need to do is create a "fail-safe loader"
disk, change the jumper configuration on the motherboard to allow FSL
and then recover my BIOS from there.

Can anybody help me out? My major problem is that I don't know which
jumper allows FSL. There appear to be none obvious on the mainboard,
but the CPU daughterboard has a number of jumpers spread over it.

Has anyone had a similar problem with these machines? If so, how did
you go about resolving the problem?

I have not yet tried the serial console... I am fairly sure the BIOS
is "dead" and needs reflashing. If anybody has an older AlphaBIOS
image known to work with these systems, a copy of that would be much
appreciated too.

Thanks in advance.


