WRONG.......   meant nicely  :^)

    Leave the first 2048 BLOCKS of the disk free.... Meaning start your
first BSD partition 2048 blocks into the disk... This is because the SRM
aboot loader resides in this area,  which is why you cannot use FAT style
partitioning, as it writes over the area used to write FAT style partition
tables...

You do not need type d partition... SRM can't use it anyway    The /boot
redhat uses is a normal ext2 partition which has a label   /boot,(if you
want one)  using their partition program.. (not fdisk) if you want it in
it's own partition.  But then the install create symbolic links between it
and /etc because aboot needs access to aboot.conf if you use aboot menu's to
select which kernel/mode (multi/single user)  to use...   7.1 uses these
label to mount these partitions to /  you can create /boot,  /usr, /var...
/etc   on different partitions on different disks if you wish.

The 7.1 install works fine,  IF you do not have a disk with partitions
already created... If not partitions are on the disk, it will create them
and install just fine..  If it finds partition tables.. it assumes you set
