"Greg Newby" <gbnewby@ruby.ils.unc.edu> wrote in message
news:9mod7c$n80$1@news2.isis.unc.edu...
> I had some trouble getting RH7.1 to work on my DP264 system,
> so wrote a little Web page about what worked for me
> (http://ils.unc.edu/gbnewby/tera/redhat71.html).
>
> Since I couldn't find this information at RedHat's support site
> (even though the tech I spoke with thought it was an FAQ), and
> didn't find this info in a search on groups.google.com either,
> I figured I'd post it here for posterity.
>
> I noted that someone was saying the "small partition" solution
> for aboot is not needed, but that's not my experience.  More
> detail, of course, would be welcome.
>
> Here's a "lynx -dump" of the Web page mentioned above:
>
>
>             Upgrade notes: Alpha DP264 to RedHat Linux 7.1
>
> Alphas are more difficult for system upgrade and installation than
> x386 (Intel/AMD) systems. This is because of the variety in
> motherboards, vendors, and boot methods.
>
> For my [1]TeraScale Retrieval project, sponsored by the National
> Science Foundation, I purchased a dual processor Alpha system from
> [2]Microway. Its specs are:
>   * 2 processors at 667 Mhz
>   * 4GB of memory
>   * 3 internal drives (2 x 18GB Cheetahs at 15Krpm; 1 x 73GB Cheetah
>     at 10Krpm)
>   * An external disk pack with another 12 73GB drives
>   * No RAID, it uses a SCSI-3 controller
>
> The system is therefore pretty compatible to Compaq's DP264 system,
> the DS20.
>
> Here's what I needed to know about re-installing the OS. This is from
> when I went from RedHat 6.2 to RedHat 7.1. I did a fresh install
> (e.g., with drive reformatting) because I wanted to start from a known
> system configuration to get Tripwire and other security stuff set up.
>
> What I needed to know is: the partition layout on the boot drive
> matters! To do a "custom" install (as opposed to a "workstation" or
> "server" configuration in RedHat's configuration screen), I did this:
>
>  1. Select "fdisk" for drive partitioning
>  2. For the boot drive:
>        + Make partition a run from cylinders 2-4, with type "d"
>          ("boot"). This is the critical part!
>        + The other partitions can have whatever layout you want, but
>          keeping a separate /boot (like you would for older x386
>          systems) is recommended by RedHat support.
>  3. I made partition b as "/boot" at 32M
>  4. I made particion c as "/" at 14GB
>  5. I made partition d as "/var" at 1.5GB
>  6. I made partition e as swap at 2GB
>
> The BSD-labeled boot-type partition was the critical part, and the
> support tech at RedHat said he hears a lot about the problem I had
> initially. Before finding out about this stuff, I went ahead and made
> a similar partition layout, without the boot-type partition a.
>
> The result was a system that would not boot! I got errors from the SRM
> or aboot manager:
>
>   ext2_bread: read error
>   aboot: read returned -1 instead of 8 byts
>   aboot: loading compressed vmlinux
>   error uncompressing vmlinuz
>   unzip: attempted to read past eof
>
> and similar errors. This happened even if I used the old (good)
> kernel, or the working kernel from the RedHat CD.
>
> Finally, I'd like to say that the RedHat support guy was quite good.
> There was only one person familiar with Alphas when I called, so I
> needed to wait on hold for about 10 minutes. But he quickly figured
> out my problem and the solution, and gave me a few other tips (such
> as, swap partitions are only used up to 2GB, so larger swap partitions
> are skipped. Only 8 swap partitions can be used). I'm quite happy with
> RedHat's support, which was included with my Alpha boxed set (cost,
> about $100).
>
